==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|aflicter}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|afflictare||to damage, harass, torment}}, frequentative of {{m|la|affligere||to dash down, overthrow}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|En-us-afflict.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
*: {{rhymes|ɪkt|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (someone) [[pain]], [[suffering]] or [[distress]].
#* '''1611''', [[w:Authorized King James Version|Authorized King James]] translation of [[w:Book of Exodus|Exodus]] 1:11–12:
#*: Therefore they did set over them taskmasters to '''afflict''' them with their burdens. And they built for Pharaoh treasure cities, Pithom and Raamses. But the more they '''afflicted''' them, the more they multiplied and grew. And they were grieved because of the children of Israel.
#* '''1611''', [[w:Authorized King James Version|Authorized King James]] translation of [[w:Book of Leviticus|Leviticus]] 23:27:
#*: Also on the tenth day of this seventh month there shall be a day of atonement: it shall be an holy convocation unto you; and ye shall '''afflict''' your souls, and offer an offering made by fire unto the LORD.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To strike or cast down; to overthrow.
#* Milton
#*: reassembling our '''afflicted''' powers
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To make low or humble.
#: {{rfquotek|Spenser}}
#* Jeremy Taylor
#*: Men are apt to prefer a prosperous error before an '''afflicted''' truth.

